INT. BELL TELEPHONE EXCHANGE - SWITCHBOARD ROOM - NIGHT

1924. Three rows of operators in headsets, a wall of brass jacks and cords. After the crisis, the room is loud with quiet. Rain on the window. Someone is crying softly.

ETHEL PARKER, 22, redhead, sits at position four. MAUDE ABERNATHY-LOWE, 30s, steady hands, position five. HATTIE JOHNSON, 19, new, position six, red-eyed, rubbing her wrist. Behind them, MISS CALLOWAY, 50s, supervisor, high collar, paces the line with a clipboard.

ETHEL
Hattie, you did right.

HATTIE
I put through the wrong call, Ethel.

MAUDE
You put through a call at the right time, Hattie. The wrong number got the right warning.

MISS CALLOWAY
Ladies, voices.

ETHEL
Yes, Miss Calloway.

A light blinks on Hattie's board. Everyone looks.

MAUDE
Hattie. Plug in.

HATTIE
I can't.

MAUDE
You can.

HATTIE
Maude, I can't.

MISS CALLOWAY
Miss Johnson. Take the call.

Hattie plugs in. Her hand trembles.

HATTIE
Number, please.

CALLER (O.S.)
(over the line)
Hello? Is this the exchange? This is Mrs. Whitmore on Elm Street. The river is up to our porch.

HATTIE
We know, ma'am.

CALLER (O.S.)
The man said to call and tell you the sirens did not sound.

ETHEL
(leaning in)
They're ringing now, ma'am.

MAUDE
Tell her to take the children upstairs.

HATTIE
Mrs. Whitmore, take the children upstairs.

CALLER (O.S.)
Yes. Yes, we will. Thank you, dear.

The line goes dead. Hattie holds her cord a moment.

MISS CALLOWAY
(softly, to Hattie)
That was well done.

HATTIE
Thank you, Miss Calloway.

ETHEL
Maude, do you think they'll be all right?

MAUDE
Most of them.

Another light blinks. Another. Hattie, Ethel, and Maude plug in together, calm, practiced. Miss Calloway watches, then lowers her clipboard and, for the first time all night, sits.
